      "reasoning_content": "Let me analyze the code to understand what needs to be tested.\n\nThe code is `DashSegmentsFD.real_download` from youtube_dl/downloader/dash.py. Lines 19-80 need coverage.\n\nKey branches to cover:\n1. `test` param True \u2192 fragments[:1] (line 20-21)\n2. `test` param False \u2192 all fragments\n3. `frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']` \u2192 continue (line 36-37)\n4. `fatal = i == 0 or not skip_unavailable_fragments` (line 40)\n5. `fragment_url = fragment.get('url')` - with url present (line 44)\n6. `if not fragment_url:` - url absent, use fragment_base_url + path (lines 45-47)\n7. `success, frag_content = self._download_fragment(...)` - success True (line 48)\n8. `if not success: return False` (lines 49-50)\n9. `self._append_fragment(ctx, frag_content)` (line 51)\n10. `except compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` - retry logic (lines 53, 60-62)\n11. `except DownloadError` - fatal vs not fatal (lines 63-69)\n12. `if count > fragment_retries:` - skip vs error (lines 71-76)\n13. `self._finish_frag_download(ctx)` (line 78)\n14. `return True` (line 80)\n\nLet me check the parent class FragmentFD to understand the methods used:\n- `_prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)`\n- `_download_fragment(ctx, fragment_url, info_dict)`\n- `_append_fragment(ctx, frag_content)`\n- `_finish_frag_download(ctx)`\n- `report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries)`\n- `report_skip_fragment(frag_index)`\n- `report_error(message)`\n\nLet me use get_info to check these methods.\n\nLet me plan the tests:\n\nTest 1: Successful download with all fragments having URLs, test=False\n- Covers lines 19-28, 30-35, 40-52, 78, 80\n- Asserts return True, fragments appended\n\nTest 2: test=True param \u2192 only first fragment downloaded\n- Covers line 20-21 branch (fragments[:1])\n\nTest 3: Fragment without 'url' key \u2192 uses fragment_base_url and path\n- Covers lines 45-47\n\nTest 4: _download_fragment returns (False, ...) \u2192 return False\n- Covers lines 49-50\n\nTest 5: HTTPError raised, retried, then success\n- Covers lines 53, 60-62 (report_retry_fragment)\n- Need fragment_retries > 0\n\nTest 6: HTTPError raised, retries exhausted \u2192 count > fragment_retries \u2192 fatal (i==0) \u2192 report_error, return False\n- Covers lines 71-76 (fatal branch)\n\nTest 7: HTTPError raised, retries exhausted, not fatal (i > 0, skip_unavailable_fragments=True) \u2192 report_skip_fragment, continue\n- Covers lines 71-74 (non-fatal branch)\n\nTest 8: DownloadError with fatal=True (i==0) \u2192 raise\n- Covers lines 63-69 (raise branch)\n\nTest 9: DownloadError with fatal=False (i>0, skip_unavailable_fragments=True) \u2192 report_skip_fragment, break\n- Covers lines 66-68\n\nTest 10: Resume: ctx['fragment_index'] >= frag_index \u2192 continue\n- Covers lines 36-37\n- Need to check how fragment_index gets into ctx. Let me check `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`.\n\nLet me look at the FragmentFD class to understand ctx initialization.\n\nI should call get_info on:\n- FragmentFD\n- _prepare_and_start_frag_download\n- _download_fragment\n- _append_fragment\n- _finish_frag_download\n- report_retry_fragment\n- report_skip_fragment\n- report_error\n\nLet me do that first.\n\nActually, let me think about how to structure the tests. I'll create a fake downloader by subclassing DashSegmentsFD and overriding the fragment methods, or by monkeypatching them.\n\nApproach: Create a real YoutubeDL-like params dict. DashSegmentsFD's __init__ takes (ydl, params). Let me check FragmentFD's constructor.\n\nFragmentFD likely inherits from FileDownloader which takes ydl and params. FileDownloader.__init__(self, ydl, params) sets self.ydl = ydl, self.params = params, etc.\n\nFor the ydl, I can use a mock with prepare_filename etc. But since we override the fragment methods, we may not need much from ydl. However, `report_error` might call `self.trouble` which calls ydl methods. Let me check.\n\nFileDownloader.report_error: `self.trouble(f'ERROR: {message}', ...)` or similar. Actually in youtube_dl, FileDownloader.report_error calls `self.trouble('[download] ERROR: %s' % message)`. And trouble calls `self.ydl.report_error(...)` unless force. Hmm, that would raise DownloadError from ydl.report_error. Let me check.\n\nActually in youtube_dl's FileDownloader:\n```python\ndef report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n    self.trouble('\\033[0;31mERROR:\\033[0m ' + message, tb, expected)\n\ndef trouble(self, message=None, tb=None, expected=False):\n    if message is not None:\n        self.to_stderr(message)\n    if self.params.get('logger'):\n        ...\n    if not self.params.get('no_progress')... \n```\n\nHmm, let me recall. Actually:\n\n```python\ndef trouble(self, message=None, tb=None, expected=False):\n    \"\"\"Determine whether to raise an error or continue\"\"\"\n    if message is not None:\n        self.to_stderr(message)\n    if self.params.get('logger'):\n        self.params['logger'].error(message)\n    if not expected:\n        if self.params.get('ignoreerrors'):\n            ...\n        else:\n            raise DownloadError(message, exc_info=tb)\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure. Let me check with get_info.\n\nActually, in youtube_dl/downloader/common.py:\n\n```python\ndef report_error(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False):\n    self.ydl.report_error(msg, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me just check with get_info.\n\nLet me plan to call get_info on the relevant symbols first, then write the tests.\n\nThe safest approach: monkeypatch `report_error`, `report_retry_fragment`, `report_skip_fragment`, `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`, `_download_fragment`, `_append_fragment`, `_finish_frag_download` on the instance or class. That way we control behavior fully and record calls.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the goal is to cover lines in `real_download` itself. The helper methods being mocked is fine; coverage of dash.py lines 19-80 is what matters.\n\nHowever, we should be careful: mocking `_download_fragment` to raise `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` \u2014 we need to construct an HTTPError. `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. We can create one with url='http://example.com', code=404, msg='Not Found', hdrs=None, fp=None.\n\nAlso `DownloadError` from youtube_dl.utils \u2014 constructor takes msg, exc_info=None. Actually `DownloadError(msg, exc_info=None)`.\n\nNow, ctx: `_prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)` normally sets ctx['fragment_index'] = 0 (or from resume). If we mock it, we need to set ctx['fragment_index'] ourselves since line 36 reads `ctx['fragment_index']`. In the real implementation:\n\n```python\ndef _prepare_and_start_frag_download(self, ctx):\n    self._prepare_frag_download(ctx)\n    self._start_frag_download(ctx)\n```\n\nAnd `_prepare_frag_download` sets ctx = {'fragment_index': 0, ...}. Actually let me check. In FragmentFD._prepare_frag_download:\n\n```python\nctx = {\n    'filename': filename,\n    'total_frags': total_frags,\n    # not really used yet\n    'fragment_index': 0,\n}\n```\n\nHmm, actually the ctx passed in already has filename and total_frags; _prepare_frag_download adds more keys. Let me check with get_info.\n\nFor the resume test (line 36-37), I can make my mocked `_prepare_and_start_frag_download` set `ctx['fragment_index'] = 1` to simulate resume, then with 2 fragments, fragment 1 is skipped (continue at line 37) and fragment 2 is downloaded. That covers line 36-37 branch.\n\nNow let me think about each test in detail:\n\nSetup helper:\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.dash import DashSegmentsFD\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import DownloadError\n```\n\nWait \u2014 is DashSegmentsFD importable from youtube_dl.downloader.dash? Yes.\n\nCreate instance:\n```python\ndef make_fd(params=None):\n    params = params or {}\n    ydl = MagicMock()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params)\n    return fd\n```\n\nBut DashSegmentsFD.__init__ \u2014 does it exist? FragmentFD.__init__? FileDownloader.__init__(self, ydl, params). Let me check whether FragmentFD defines __init__. Probably not; FileDownloader's is used. FileDownloader.__init__:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, ydl, params):\n    self.ydl = ydl\n    self.params = params\n    self._progress_hooks = []\n    ...\n```\n\nSomething like that. A MagicMock ydl should be fine.\n\nNow, mocking methods. I'll use monkeypatch.setattr on the instance:\n\n```python\ncalls = []\ndef fake_prepare(ctx):\n    ctx['fragment_index'] = 0\n    calls.append(('prepare', dict(ctx)))\nmonkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare)\n```\n\nWait, but monkeypatch.setattr on an instance with a plain function: `monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare)` sets an instance attribute, which shadows the class method. Since it's not bound, calling `self._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)` will call fake_prepare(ctx) \u2014 correct, no self passed. Good.\n\nAlternatively use pytest-mock's mocker. The instructions say use monkeypatch or pytest-mock if appropriate. I'll use monkeypatch.\n\nTest 1: full success, fragments with 'url' key.\n\n```python\ndef test_full_download_success(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {})\n    events = []\n    appended = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', lambda ctx: ctx.update(fragment_index=0))\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info_dict):\n        events.append(('download', url))\n        return True, b'data-' + url.encode()\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', fake_download)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_append_fragment', lambda ctx, content: appended.append(content))\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_finish_frag_download', lambda ctx: events.append(('finish',)))\n    \n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/f1'}, {'url': 'http://example.com/f2'}],\n    }\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert events == [('download', 'http://example.com/f1'), ('download', 'http://example.com/f2'), ('finish',)]\n    assert appended == [b'data-http://example.com/f1', b'data-http://example.com/f2']\n```\n\nThis covers lines 19-28, 30-35, 40-52, 78, 80.\n\nTest 2: test=True \u2192 only first fragment.\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {'test': True})\n    ...\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}, {'url': 'u2'}, {'url': 'u3'}]}\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    # only u1 downloaded\n```\n\nTest 3: fragment without url \u2192 urljoin(fragment_base_url, path).\n\n```python\ndef test_fragment_path_urljoin(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {})\n    urls = []\n    ...\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragment_base_url': 'http://base/',\n        'fragments': [{'path': 'seg1.m4s'}],\n    }\n    ...\n    assert urls == ['http://base/seg1.m4s']\n```\n\nurljoin('http://base/', 'seg1.m4s') \u2192 'http://base/seg1.m4s'. Good.\n\nTest 4: _download_fragment returns False \u2192 return False.\n\n```python\ndef test_download_fragment_failure_returns_false(monkeypatch):\n    ...\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', lambda ctx, url, info: (False, None))\n    result = fd.real_download('f', {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}]})\n    assert result is False\n    # _append_fragment not called, _finish not called\n```\n\nTest 5: HTTPError then success (retry works).\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retry_then_success(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {'fragment_retries': 2})\n    retries = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_retry_fragment', lambda err, fi, count, retries_total: retries.append((fi, count, retries_total)))\n    attempts = {'n': 0}\n    err = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://x', 404, 'Not Found', None, None)\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info):\n        attempts['n'] += 1\n        if attempts['n'] == 1:\n            raise err\n        return True, b'data'\n    ...\n    result = fd.real_download('f', {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}]})\n    assert result is True\n    assert attempts['n'] == 2\n    assert retries == [(1, 1, 2)]\n```\n\nWait \u2014 check the retry loop logic: count starts 0. while count <= fragment_retries (2): try download \u2192 raises HTTPError \u2192 count += 1 \u2192 count=1 <= 2 \u2192 report_retry_fragment(err, 1, 1, 2). Loop again: count=1 <= 2 \u2192 try \u2192 success \u2192 break. Good. So retries == [(1, 1, 2)].\n\nTest 6: HTTPError exhausts retries, fatal (first fragment) \u2192 report_error, return False.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_exhausted_fatal(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {'fragment_retries': 1})\n    errors = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_error', lambda msg: errors.append(msg))\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info):\n        raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://x', 404, 'Not Found', None, None)\n    ...\n    result = fd.real_download('f', {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}]})\n    assert result is False\n    assert errors == ['giving up after 1 fragment retries']\n```\n\nTrace: count=0, while 0<=1: raise \u2192 count=1, 1<=1 \u2192 report_retry_fragment (need to mock it too, or it will call the real one which calls to_screen \u2192 ydl.to_screen; MagicMock ydl handles that fine actually). Hmm, real report_retry_fragment calls self.to_screen which calls self.ydl.to_screen \u2014 with MagicMock ydl that's fine. But to be safe and to assert, I'll mock it.\n\nLoop: count=1 <= 1 \u2192 try \u2192 raise \u2192 count=2, 2<=1 false \u2192 no report. Exit loop. count=2 > 1 \u2192 fatal (i==0) \u2192 report_error('giving up after 1 fragment retries') \u2192 return False. \n\nTest 7: HTTPError exhausts retries, not fatal (second fragment, skip_unavailable default True) \u2192 report_skip_fragment, continue, then finish.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_exhausted_skip(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {'fragment_retries': 0})\n    skipped = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_skip_fragment', lambda fi: skipped.append(fi))\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info):\n        if url == 'u2':\n            raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(...)\n        return True, b'data'\n    info = {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}, {'url': 'u2'}]}\n    result = fd.real_download('f', info)\n    assert result is True\n    assert skipped == [2]\n```\n\nTrace: frag 1 downloads fine. frag 2: count=0, while 0<=0: raise \u2192 count=1, 1<=0 false. Exit. count=1 > 0 \u2192 fatal = (i==0)=False or not True=False \u2192 not fatal \u2192 report_skip_fragment(2) \u2192 continue. Then finish, return True. \n\nTest 8: DownloadError, fatal (first fragment) \u2192 raise.\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_fatal_raises(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {})\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info):\n        raise DownloadError('boom')\n    ...\n    with pytest.raises(DownloadError):\n        fd.real_download('f', {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}]})\n```\n\nNote: fatal = i==0 \u2192 True. DownloadError \u2192 not fatal is False \u2192 raise. Good. Also need to make sure _finish_frag_download not called.\n\nTest 9: DownloadError, not fatal \u2192 report_skip_fragment, break, continue to next fragment.\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_nonfatal_skips(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {})\n    skipped = []\n    downloaded = []\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info):\n        if url == 'u1':\n            raise DownloadError('boom')\n        downloaded.append(url)\n        return True, b'data'\n    info = {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}, {'url': 'u2'}]}\n    result = fd.real_download('f', info)\n    assert result is True\n    assert skipped == [1]\n    assert downloaded == ['u2']\n```\n\nTrace: frag 1 (i=0): fatal=True. DownloadError \u2192 not fatal False \u2192 raise?? Wait, i=0 \u2192 fatal=True \u2192 raise. Hmm, I need the failing fragment to be i>0. Let me fix: u1 succeeds, u2 raises DownloadError. i=1, skip_unavailable_fragments=True \u2192 fatal = False or not True = False \u2192 not fatal \u2192 report_skip_fragment(2) \u2192 break. Then count=0 which is not > fragment_retries (0) \u2192 continue loop \u2192 finish \u2192 True.\n\nSo:\n```python\ndef fake_download(ctx, url, info):\n    if url == 'u2':\n        raise DownloadError('boom')\n    return True, b'data'\n```\nskipped == [2], downloaded == ['u1'].\n\nTest 10: resume \u2014 fragment_index set by prepare \u2192 skip first fragment.\n\n```python\ndef test_resume_skips_downloaded_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {})\n    downloaded = []\n    def fake_prepare(ctx):\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = 1  # first fragment already downloaded\n    ...\n    info = {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}, {'url': 'u2'}]}\n    result = fd.real_download('f', info)\n    assert result is True\n    assert downloaded == ['u2']\n```\n\nTrace: frag_index=1 for first fragment: 1 <= 1 \u2192 continue. Second: frag_index=2 > 1 \u2192 download. Good, covers lines 36-37.\n\nTest 11: skip_unavailable_fragments=False with DownloadError on non-first fragment \u2192 fatal \u2192 raise.\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_no_skip_raises(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {'skip_unavailable_fragments': False})\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info):\n        if url == 'u2':\n            raise DownloadError('boom')\n        return True, b'data'\n    with pytest.raises(DownloadError):\n        fd.real_download('f', {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}, {'url': 'u2'}]})\n```\n\nThis covers the `not skip_unavailable_fragments` part of line 40 and the raise branch at line 69 via non-first fragment.\n\nAlso, maybe test HTTPError exhausted with skip_unavailable_fragments=False on non-first fragment \u2192 fatal \u2192 report_error + return False. That covers line 40's second operand in the count>fragment_retries branch. Let me add:\n\nTest 12: HTTPError exhausted, skip_unavailable_fragments=False, non-first fragment \u2192 report_error, return False.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_exhausted_no_skip_fatal(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {'fragment_retries': 0, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': False})\n    errors = []\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_error', lambda msg: errors.append(msg))\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info):\n        if url == 'u2':\n            raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://x', 500, 'Server Error', None, None)\n        return True, b'data'\n    result = fd.real_download('f', {'fragments': [{'url': 'u1'}, {'url': 'u2'}]})\n    assert result is False\n    assert errors == ['giving up after 0 fragment retries']\n```\n\nHmm wait, fragment_retries=0: count=0, while 0<=0: raise \u2192 count=1, 1<=0 false. Exit. count=1 > 0 \u2192 fatal (not skip_unavailable \u2192 True) \u2192 report_error \u2192 return False. Good.\n\nAlso, what about the case where fragment has no 'url' and no fragment_base_url \u2192 assert fails (AssertionError). Line 46: `assert fragment_base_url`. Should I test that? It's a branch (assert). The assert statement itself is line 46; if fragment_url is falsy we execute line 46. If fragment_base_url is truthy, line 47 executes. If falsy, AssertionError. To cover line 46 fully (the assert check), having a fragment without url and with fragment_base_url set executes line 46 (evaluates it) and line 47. The AssertionError branch would also be nice but it's an assert \u2014 coverage-wise, line 46 is covered either way. I could add a test for the AssertionError for completeness:\n\nTest 13: fragment without url and without fragment_base_url \u2192 AssertionError.\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_base_url_asserts(monkeypatch):\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), {})\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError):\n        fd.real_download('f', {'fragments': [{'path': 'seg'}]})\n```\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 AssertionError raised inside the try block... The except clauses catch HTTPError and DownloadError. AssertionError propagates. Good.\n\nActually, is it worth it? It does exercise line 46's failing branch. I'll include it \u2014 it's cheap.\n\nNow, about the ydl mock: FileDownloader.__init__ might call things on ydl. Let me check what FileDownloader.__init__ does in youtube_dl:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, ydl, params):\n    \"\"\"Create a FileDownloader object.\"\"\"\n    self.ydl = ydl\n    self._progress_hooks = []\n    self.params = params\n    self._retcode = 0\n    self._interrupted = False\n    self._interrupt_trigger = ...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me check with get_info. Actually, in youtube_dl (the older project), FileDownloader.__init__:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, ydl, params):\n    \"\"\"Create a FileDownloader object.\"\"\"\n    self.ydl = ydl\n    self._progress_hooks = []\n    self.params = params\n    self._last_progress_update = 0\n    self._interrupted = False\n```\n\nSomething like that. It doesn't call ydl methods I think. Actually it might call `self.report_progress`... no. Let me just check.\n\nAlso, `to_screen` in common.py:\n```python\ndef to_screen(self, message, skip_eol=False, check_quiet=False):\n    \"\"\"Print message to stdout if not in quiet mode\"\"\"\n    if self.params.get('logger'):\n        self.params['logger'].debug(message)\n    ...\n    else:\n        self.ydl.to_screen(...)\n```\n\nSince I'm mocking report_* methods in most tests, to_screen won't be called. But in tests where I don't mock report_retry_fragment (e.g., test 6 exhausts retries and calls report_retry_fragment at count=1)... I said I'd mock it. Let me make sure every test mocks all report_* and _* methods that could hit ydl, to be safe. Actually, using a MagicMock ydl means even if real methods are called, they'd work (MagicMock returns MagicMock for anything). But real `to_screen` might do string formatting \u2014 fine with MagicMock.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: FileDownloader.to_screen might check `self.params.get('quiet')`. With params dict lacking 'quiet', it calls self.ydl.to_screen(...) which is a MagicMock \u2014 fine.\n\nBut actually, wait: does DashSegmentsFD's real_download call anything else? `self.report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries)` \u2014 real implementation:\n\n```python\ndef report_retry_fragment(self, err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries):\n    self.to_screen('[download] Got error HTTP error %s ... Retrying (attempt %d of %s) ...' % (...))\n```\n\nFine with MagicMock ydl.\n\n`report_skip_fragment`:\n```python\ndef report_skip_fragment(self, frag_index):\n    self.to_screen('[download] Skipping fragment %d ...' % frag_index)\n```\n\nFine.\n\n`report_error` \u2014 this is the dangerous one. In FileDownloader:\n\n```python\ndef report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n    self.ydl.report_error(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm, or does it call self.trouble? Let me check. In youtube_dl/downloader/common.py:\n\n```python\ndef report_error(self, msg, tb=None, expected=False):\n    \"\"\"Like report_warning but fail the download\"\"\"\n    self.ydl.report_error(msg, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure whether it's in common.py or FileDownloader. If it calls self.ydl.report_error with a MagicMock ydl, it just records the call and doesn't raise. That's actually convenient \u2014 but to be deterministic, I'll mock report_error on the fd instance anyway.\n\nActually wait \u2014 if report_error calls self.ydl.report_error and ydl is a MagicMock, then no exception is raised, and real_download returns False. Good. But I'll mock it to capture the message for assertions.\n\nLet me now check the actual source with get_info to be safe. I'll query:\n- 'DashSegmentsFD' (class info)\n- 'FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download'\n- 'FragmentFD._download_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD._append_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD._finish_frag_download'\n- 'FileDownloader.report_error'\n- 'FileDownloader.__init__'\n- 'FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment'\n- 'FragmentFD.report_skip_fragment'\n\nLet me make these calls in parallel.\n\nActually, the get_info tool returns \"information about a symbol\" \u2014 presumably source code or signature. Let me call it for the key ones.\n\nThen write the test file.\n\nOne more consideration: the ctx dict. real_download creates ctx with 'filename' and 'total_frags', then calls _prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx). My mocked prepare sets ctx['fragment_index']. In the real code, _prepare_frag_download does:\n\n```python\nctx.update({\n    'tmpfilename': ...,\n    'fragment_index': 0,\n    ...\n})\n```\n\nActually I recall in FragmentFD._prepare_frag_download:\n\n```python\ndef _prepare_frag_download(self, ctx):\n    if 'live' not in ctx:\n        ctx['live'] = False\n    if not ctx['live'] and (not ctx['tmpfilename'] ...):\n    ...\n    ctx.update({\n        'downloaded_bytes': 0,\n        'frag_index': 0,  # hmm\n        'fragment_index': 0,\n        ...\n    })\n```\n\nWhatever \u2014 since I mock it, I control it. The key: line 36 reads ctx['fragment_index'], so my mock must set it (or the real one would). For tests where I don't care, setting fragment_index=0 in the mock prepare is the right simulation.\n\nLet me also double check `urljoin` from youtube_dl.utils \u2014 it's compat's urljoin (urllib.parse.urljoin). urljoin('http://base/', 'seg1.m4s') = 'http://base/seg1.m4s'. Good.\n\nNow about imports in the test: \n\n```python\nfrom unittest.mock import MagicMock\nimport pytest\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.dash import DashSegmentsFD\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import DownloadError\n```\n\nCreating HTTPError: `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)` \u2014 hdrs and fp can be None. HTTPError is a subclass of URLError and OSError. Constructing with fp=None is fine as long as we don't call .read() etc. The code only passes err to report_retry_fragment. Good.\n\nDownloadError: `DownloadError(msg, exc_info=None)` \u2014 from youtube_dl.utils:\n\n```python\nclass DownloadError(YoutubeDLError):\n    \"\"\"Download Error exception.\n\n    This exception may be thrown by FileDownloader objects if they are not\n    configured to continue on errors. They will just contain a string\n    representation of the exception.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, msg, exc_info=None):\n        \"\"\" exc_info, if given, is the original exception (as from sys.exc_info()), and it is normally more specific than msg.\n        \"\"\"\n        super(DownloadError, self).__init__(msg)\n        self.exc_info = exc_info\n```\n\nGood, DownloadError('boom') works.\n\nNow, the fixture/helper. I'll write a helper function that creates the fd and installs common mocks, returning a recorder object. But instructions say \"Write as little top-level code as possible\" \u2014 helpers are fine, just no top-level execution code (no pytest.main, no direct calls).\n\nLet me design a helper:\n\n```python\nclass Recorder:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.prepared = []\n        self.downloads = []\n        self.appended = []\n        self.finished = []\n        self.retries = []\n        self.skipped = []\n        self.errors = []\n\n\ndef make_fd(monkeypatch, params=None, rec=None, prepare_hook=None, download_fn=None):\n    rec = rec or Recorder()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(MagicMock(), params or {})\n\n    def _prepare(ctx):\n        rec.prepared.append(dict(ctx))\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = 0\n        if prepare_hook:\n            prepare_hook(ctx)\n\n    def _download(ctx, url, info_dict):\n        rec.downloads.append(url)\n        if download_fn is not None:\n            return download_fn(ctx, url, info_dict)\n        return True, b'frag-' + url.encode('utf-8')\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', _prepare)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', _download)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_append_fragment', lambda ctx, content: rec.appended.append(content))\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_finish_frag_download', lambda ctx: rec.finished.append(dict(ctx)))\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_retry_fragment', lambda err, fi, count, retries: rec.retries.append((fi, count, retries)))\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_skip_fragment', lambda fi: rec.skipped.append(fi))\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_error', lambda msg, *a, **k: rec.errors.append(msg))\n    return fd, rec\n```\n\nHmm, report_error signature: real one might be `report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False)`. My lambda with *a, **k handles it.\n\nWait, one issue: monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_error', ...) \u2014 setting an instance attribute on the fd object. monkeypatch will delete it on teardown. Fine.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 is `report_error` defined on FileDownloader or common? If it's not defined on the class, setattr on instance still works (creates instance attr). monkeypatch.setattr requires the attribute to exist by default (raising=False not set). If report_error doesn't exist anywhere in the MRO, monkeypatch.setattr raises AttributeError. Let me verify with get_info that FileDownloader.report_error exists. It should \u2014 FileDownloader has report_error. Let me confirm via get_info.\n\nSimilarly report_retry_fragment and report_skip_fragment are on FragmentFD. _prepare_and_start_frag_download, _download_fragment, _append_fragment, _finish_frag_download on FragmentFD. Let me verify all with get_info.\n\nNow the tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_success_all_fragments_with_urls(monkeypatch):\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch)\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}, {'url': 'http://e/2'}, {'url': 'http://e/3'}]}\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', info_dict) is True\n    assert rec.downloads == ['http://e/1', 'http://e/2', 'http://e/3']\n    assert rec.appended == [b'frag-http://e/1', b'frag-http://e/2', b'frag-http://e/3']\n    assert rec.finished and not rec.errors and not rec.skipped\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_downloads_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, params={'test': True})\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}, {'url': 'http://e/2'}]}\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', info_dict) is True\n    assert rec.downloads == ['http://e/1']\n    assert len(rec.finished) == 1\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_fragment_without_url_uses_base_url_and_path(monkeypatch):\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch)\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragment_base_url': 'http://base/vid/',\n        'fragments': [{'path': 'init.mp4'}, {'path': 'seg1.m4s'}],\n    }\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', info_dict) is True\n    assert rec.downloads == ['http://base/vid/init.mp4', 'http://base/vid/seg1.m4s']\n```\n\nurljoin('http://base/vid/', 'init.mp4') \u2192 'http://base/vid/init.mp4'. Good.\n\n```python\ndef test_download_fragment_failure_returns_false(monkeypatch):\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, download_fn=lambda ctx, url, info: (False, None))\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}, {'url': 'http://e/2'}]}\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', info_dict) is False\n    assert rec.downloads == ['http://e/1']\n    assert rec.appended == []\n    assert rec.finished == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retried_then_success(monkeypatch):\n    err = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://e/1', 404, 'Not Found', None, None)\n    state = {'calls': 0}\n\n    def download_fn(ctx, url, info):\n        state['calls'] += 1\n        if state['calls'] == 1:\n            raise err\n        return True, b'frag'\n\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, params={'fragment_retries': 3}, download_fn=download_fn)\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}]}) is True\n    assert state['calls'] == 2\n    assert rec.retries == [(1, 1, 3)]\n    assert rec.appended == [b'frag']\n    assert rec.finished and not rec.errors and not rec.skipped\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_exhausted_retries_fatal_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    def download_fn(ctx, url, info):\n        raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://e/1', 404, 'Not Found', None, None)\n\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, params={'fragment_retries': 1}, download_fn=download_fn)\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}]}) is False\n    assert rec.errors == ['giving up after 1 fragment retries']\n    assert rec.retries == [(1, 1, 1)]\n    assert rec.appended == []\n    assert rec.finished == []\n```\n\nTrace: count=0: raise \u2192 count=1, 1<=1 \u2192 report_retry_fragment(1,1,1). count=1: raise \u2192 count=2, 2<=1 false. Exit. count=2>1 \u2192 fatal \u2192 report_error \u2192 return False. retries == [(1,1,1)]. Good.\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_exhausted_retries_skips_unavailable_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    def download_fn(ctx, url, info):\n        if url == 'http://e/2':\n            raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://e/2', 500, 'Server Error', None, None)\n        return True, b'frag-' + url.encode()\n\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, params={'fragment_retries': 0}, download_fn=download_fn)\n    info = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}, {'url': 'http://e/2'}]}\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', info) is True\n    assert rec.skipped == [2]\n    assert rec.appended == [b'frag-http://e/1']\n    assert rec.finished and not rec.errors\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_exhausted_retries_no_skip_is_fatal(monkeypatch):\n    def download_fn(ctx, url, info):\n        if url == 'http://e/2':\n            raise compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://e/2', 500, 'Server Error', None, None)\n        return True, b'frag-' + url.encode()\n\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, params={'fragment_retries': 0, 'skip_unavailable_fragments': False}, download_fn=download_fn)\n    info = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}, {'url': 'http://e/2'}]}\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', info) is False\n    assert rec.errors == ['giving up after 0 fragment retries']\n    assert rec.finished == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_on_first_fragment_is_fatal(monkeypatch):\n    def download_fn(ctx, url, info):\n        raise DownloadError('boom')\n\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, download_fn=download_fn)\n    with pytest.raises(DownloadError):\n        fd.real_download('out.mp4', {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}]})\n    assert rec.finished == []\n    assert rec.appended == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_skips_unavailable_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    def download_fn(ctx, url, info):\n        if url == 'http://e/2':\n            raise DownloadError('boom')\n        return True, b'frag-' + url.encode()\n\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, download_fn=download_fn)\n    info = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}, {'url': 'http://e/2'}, {'url': 'http://e/3'}]}\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', info) is True\n    assert rec.skipped == [2]\n    assert rec.appended == [b'frag-http://e/1', b'frag-http://e/3']\n    assert rec.finished and not rec.errors\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_no_skip_raises(monkeypatch):\n    def download_fn(ctx, url, info):\n        if url == 'http://e/2':\n            raise DownloadError('boom')\n        return True, b'frag-' + url.encode()\n\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, params={'skip_unavailable_fragments': False}, download_fn=download_fn)\n    info = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}, {'url': 'http://e/2'}]}\n    with pytest.raises(DownloadError):\n        fd.real_download('out.mp4', info)\n    assert rec.appended == [b'frag-http://e/1']\n    assert rec.finished == []\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_resume_skips_already_downloaded_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    def prepare_hook(ctx):\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = 2  # first two fragments already present\n\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch, prepare_hook=prepare_hook)\n    info = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://e/1'}, {'url': 'http://e/2'}, {'url': 'http://e/3'}]}\n    assert fd.real_download('out.mp4', info) is True\n    assert rec.downloads == ['http://e/3']\n    assert rec.appended == [b'frag-http://e/3']\n    assert rec.finished\n```\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_fragment_url_and_base_url_asserts(monkeypatch):\n    fd, rec = make_fd(monkeypatch)\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError):\n        fd.real_download('out.mp4', {'fragments': [{'path': 'seg.m4s'}]})\n    assert rec.downloads == []\n    assert rec.finished == []\n```\n\nHmm wait \u2014 in this last test, the AssertionError happens inside the while loop's try. The except clauses don't catch AssertionError, so it propagates out of real_download. Good.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 one subtlety with the assert: if Python is run with -O, asserts are stripped. Tests shouldn't assume -O. Fine.\n\nNow, coverage check \u2014 which lines/branches do these tests hit?\n\nLine 19: fragment_base_url = info_dict.get('fragment_base_url') \u2014 all tests.\nLine 20-21: both branches (test=True in test 2; test=False elsewhere).\nLine 23-25: ctx creation \u2014 all.\nLine 28: _prepare_and_start_frag_download \u2014 all.\nLine 30-31: params.get \u2014 all (various params).\nLine 33-35: loop \u2014 all.\nLine 36-37: continue branch \u2014 test 10 (resume). Non-continue branch \u2014 others.\nLine 40: fatal computation \u2014 both operands: i==0 (many), not skip_unavailable_fragments (tests 8/12).\nLine 41-42: while loop \u2014 all.\nLine 44: fragment.get('url') \u2014 all.\nLine 45-46-47: not fragment_url branch \u2014 tests 3 and 13. With url \u2014 others.\nLine 48: _download_fragment \u2014 all.\nLine 49-50: not success \u2192 return False \u2014 test 4.\nLine 51: _append_fragment \u2014 success tests.\nLine 52: break \u2014 success tests.\nLine 53, 60: HTTPError handler \u2014 tests 5, 6, 7, 8.\nLine 61-62: count <= fragment_retries \u2192 report_retry_fragment \u2014 tests 5, 6. The false branch (count > fragment_retries after increment) \u2014 tests 7, 8 (fragment_retries=0,